findings.body:
Password reset tokens must be unpredictable. This replaces `randomBytes(32)` (256 bits of CSPRNG output) with `Math.floor(Math.random() * 1e12).toString(36)`, which is neither cryptographically secure nor large enough: at most ~10^12 values (~40 bits), often only 9 base36 characters after `padStart`.

An attacker who can request resets can brute-force or predict tokens and take over accounts. `Math.random` is the wrong fix for edge compatibility.

Use the Web Crypto API, available on edge runtimes and modern Node:

```suggestion
export function newResetToken(): string {
  const bytes = new Uint8Array(32);
  crypto.getRandomValues(bytes);
  return Array.from(bytes, (b) => b.toString(16).padStart(2, "0")).join("");
}
```
